Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. *This is not legal advice. I do
not offer legal advice. If you need legal advice seek legal counsel.
Please be advised, the information shared in this guide is for
educational and  informational purposes only.* 

DISPUTE 21 - DAY 2
Understanding the Debt Validation Process

Bruce, It's highly likely that the majority of the consumer
reports you or your processors review on a daily or weekly basis will
contain at least one collection account. In today's lesson we're
going to continue to look at Debt Validation to help you tackle these
type of accounts and gain an understanding of what the law requires
before a collection agency can collect. 

When reviewing and discussing a client's consumer report, usually
during the initial consultation or during the analysis appointment, you
will have the conversation where you inform them that as a repair
specialist you are trained to identify the inaccurate reporting of
information on the credit report. You are also explaining that although
an account may belong to them, if it is inaccurate it isn't allowed, by
law, to be on their consumer report. 

When addressing any account, you always want to gather information about
the account from the client and make notes of their recollection of the
account before beginning the dispute process 

**(having this conversation covers you in case the client comes back
later and says that they never agreed for you to dispute an account)**.
During this conversation a client might ask if they should just pay the
default balance and get it over with, particularly if they know the debt
is theirs and they are needing to pay it off to have a loan application
approved. In some cases, depending on the time frame, paying the debt
may be the best option. However, in most cases, there are some very good
reasons to exercise your right to request validation of the debt and you
would want to communicate this to your client instead of simply advising
them not to pay the debt and just proceeding with the repair process. 

Remember, educating the client is a vital part of the repair process.
We're not just repairing their credit, we're restoring their mind so
that they can continue to benefit from their good credit and not find
themselves back in the same predicament months later. 

Below are ways to explain this process to the client as well as reasons
for understanding why we as repair professionals request Validation.  

Why Request Validation Instead of Just Paying

1.     We want to verify the debt actually belongs to the client:
Debt collectors have been known to send bills or make calls for bogus
debts, so when explaining why the client may want to request validation
instead of paying, explain to them that we never want to assume that a
bill from a debt collector automatically means they owe. The letter may
look legitimate, but in this information age, it's easy to gather enough
information about a person and their financial dealings to create a fake
debt collection notice. When you are requesting validation, you can make
mention of this in your request for information. 

2.     We want to confirm that the client hasn't already paid:
What if your client has already paid the debt? They may vaguely remember
owing the creditor named on the collection notice or they may recall
paying that debt at some point. To be certain, we're going to ask for
proof. Explain to the client that it's their right.

3.     We want to force the collector to prove the debt is real:
Sometimes collectors resurrect old debts in an attempt to make some
money. With old debts, there's a good chance the collector doesn't have
the original documents proving that the client even owes what they are
claiming. Ask your client, "Would you really pay money to someone who
says you owe them, but can't prove you owe?" Of course, not.

4.     We want to make sure the collector is authorized to collect
the debt: Even if your client really owes the money, how do we know the
creditor actually hired this company to collect the debt on their
behalf? What if your client paid the debt collector and the creditor or
another collector came after them anyway because the collector was never
hired in the first place? Sending a debt validation letter would help
them to be sure they're paying the right company for the right debt.
Things You Need to Know About Debt Validation As 
The Repair Specialist

Debt Validation is Time-Sensitive

Within five days of its initial communication to the consumer the debt
collector is required to send a written debt validation notice. Please
note, it is possible for the debt validation notice to be included in
the first communication. Under the FDCPA, this is allowed as long as the
notice is made in writing. But, if the debt collector's first
communication with you is a phone call, you should receive a debt
validation letter from them within five days. 

The collector's debt validation notice to the consumer should include
the following:
* amount of the debt

* the name of the creditor

* the assumption that the debt will be valid unless you dispute it
within 30 days

* notification that you can request verification of the debt within 30
days

* notification that you can request the name and address of the original
creditor within 30 days

During the 30-day period, the collector can continue attempts to collect
the debt from the consumer until it receives the validation request.

Submitting a Validation Request

To be legally valid, the request for proof must be made in writing. A
verbal phone request for debt validation is not enough to protect the
consumer rights under the FDCPA. In the validation letter, you can
dispute the entire debt, part of the debt, or request the name of the
original creditor. Once the debt collector receives the validation
request, they cannot contact your client again until they've provided
them with the proof they've asked for.

Most times when we as credit repair specialist are requesting validation
of a debt, the debt is beyond the 30 day debt validation notification
phase, commonly known as the Dunning Period. However, sometimes we will
get a client who has just received a notice or we have current clients
who are contacted by new collection companies while they are going
through the repair process. In this instance, the best way to send your
debt validation request is via certified mail with return receipt
requested. This way, you have proof that the letter was mailed, the date
you mailed it, and you can check to see when the debt collector received
your letter. If you have to file a lawsuit against the debt collector,
the certified and return receipts will help strengthen your case. You
can show that you mailed the letter within the 30-day time frame and
that the collector received it.

The Collector's Response

After receiving your dispute, the collection agency must send you proof
that it owns or has been assigned the debt by the original creditor.
Verification that you owe the debt and the amount of the debt needs to
include documentation from original creditor (you'll receive it from the
debt collector, not the original creditor). If you remember from
yesterday's lesson, it is not enough for the collection agency to
simply send you a printout of the amount owed.

If the debt collector does not verify the debt, it is not allowed to
continue collecting the debt from you nor can it sue you or list the
debt on your credit report. You can dispute the debt with the credit
bureaus if the collector continues to list the debt on your credit
report even though it hasn't responded to your debt validation noticed.
Send the credit bureau a copy of your debt validation letter along with
the certified and return receipts to help get the account removed from
the credit report. 

If the Collector Verifies the Debt

If you receive sufficient validation of the debt, you have to decide
what to do next. 

The first thing you would want to do is confirm the client's debt is
within the statute of limitation - that's the amount of time a creditor
or collector can use the courts to collect a debt from you. A debt
that's outside the statute of limitation poses less of a threat to you
since the collector can't win a judgment against you in court (as long
as you can prove the statute of limitations has passed).

The next thing you want to do is check to see whether the debt is still
within the credit reporting time limit. You will do this by checking the
payment history to determine the date of first delinquency. This is the
first 30 day missed payment date that led to the account going into
charge-off/collection status. Even if the client has made a payment on
the account since that first 30 day late that led to the account
closing, if they did not bring the account all the way current then the
date of first delinquency does not change just because they may have
made a payment. Most negative information, such as a collection account,
can only remain on the credit reports up to 7 ½ years from the date of
first delinquency. 

If the debt collection is old and scheduled to be removed from the
client's credit report in less than two years, you may just want to
focus on other accounts and let it fall off the credit report
organically. Negative accounts older than 5 years are not impacting the
score nearly as much as newer accounts. Even after going a few rounds,
once an aged negative account is removed it is unlikely that you'll
see a huge jump in the score. So it may be best to focus your efforts
elsewhere, especially if your client isn't planning to get a major
loan in that time period. In the case of a client preparing to get a
mortgage, tackling older accounts may benefit them no matter how much
they are affecting the score as some lenders and programs will only
allow an applicant to have a certain amount of negative debt reporting.
This amount is usually under $1,000.

What if the debt collection has been verified and is within the statute
of limitations or the credit reporting time limit? You may want to begin
to discuss with your client the option to settle the account with the
collector for a percentage of the amount owed or to offer a pay for
delete agreement.

Resources:
See Statute of Limitations By State

, Fair Debt Collection Practices Act

That's all for Day 2, but We're Just Getting Started!!!

In upcoming lessons we will cover topics such as: 

- The Debt Validation Request AFTER the 30 day period
- Pay for Delete and other methods to settle accounts that Are
Validated     and Verified
- Consumer Laws: FCRA, FDCPA, FCBA
- Case Law
- Dispute letter writing
- Clearly Written Dispute Reasons and how to PROPERLY Factually  
   Dispute
- Disputing Personal Information
- The Dispute Process in 5 Easy Steps
- Advanced Dispute Tactics
