ize: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: =
inherit; font-family: arial;"><br style=3D"">After receiving your dispute, =
the collection agency must send you proof that it owns or has been assigned=
 the debt by the original creditor. Verification that you owe the debt and =
the amount of the debt needs to include documentation from original credito=
r (you'll receive it from the debt collector, not the original creditor). I=
f you remember from yesterday=E2=80=99s lesson, it is not=0Aenough for the =
collection agency to simply send you a printout of the amount owed.<br styl=
e=3D""></span><br style=3D""><span style=3D"color: #000000; font-size: inhe=
rit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inherit; =
font-family: arial;">If the debt collector does not verify the debt, it is =
not allowed to continue collecting the debt from you nor can it sue you or =
list the debt on your credit report. You can dispute the debt with the cred=
it bureaus if the collector continues to list the debt on your credit repor=
t even though it hasn't responded to your debt validation noticed. Send the=
 credit bureau a copy of your debt validation letter along with the certifi=
ed and return receipts to help get the account removed from the credit repo=
rt. </span><br style=3D""><b style=3D"margin: 0; outline: none; padding: 0;=
