=3D""></span></b><br style=3D""><span style=3D"color: #000000; font-size: i=
nherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; text-decoration: inheri=
t; font-family: arial;">If you receive sufficient validation of the debt, y=
ou have to decide what to do next. </span><br style=3D""><span style=3D"col=
or: #000000; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit=
; text-decoration: inherit; font-family: arial;"><br style=3D"">The first t=
hing you would want to do is confirm the client=E2=80=99s debt is within th=
e statute of limitation - that's the amount of time a creditor or collector=
 can use the courts to collect a debt from you. A debt that's outside the s=
tatute of limitation poses less of a threat to you since the collector can'=
t win a judgment against you in court (as long as you can prove the statute=
 of limitations has passed).</span><br style=3D""><span style=3D"color: #00=
0000; font-size: inherit;=0Afont-weight: inherit; line-height: inherit; tex=
t-decoration: inherit; font-family: arial;"><br style=3D"">The next thing y=
ou want to do is check to see whether the debt is still within the credit r=
eporting time limit. You will do this by checking the payment history to de=
termine the date of first delinquency. This is the first 30 day missed paym=
ent date that led to the account going into charge-off/collection status. E=
ven if the client has made a payment on the account since that first 30 day=
 late that led to the account closing, if they did not bring the account al=
l the way current then the date of first delinquency does not change just b=
ecause they may have made a payment. Most negative information, such as a c=
ollection account, can only remain on the credit reports up to 7 =C2=BD yea=
rs from the date of first delinquency. </span><br style=3D""><span style=3D=
"color: #000000; font-size: inherit; font-weight: inherit; line-height: inh=
erit; text-decoration: inherit;=0Afont-family: arial;"><br style=3D"">If th=
e debt collection is old and scheduled to be removed from the client=
=E2=80=99s credit report in less than two years, you may just want to focus=
 on other accounts and let it fall off the credit report organically. Negat=
ive accounts older than 5 years are not impacting the score nearly as much =
as newer accounts. Even after going a few rounds, once an aged negative acc=
ount is removed it is unlikely that you=E2=80=99ll see a huge jump in the s=
core. So it may be best to focus your efforts elsewhere, especially if your=
 client isn=E2=80=99t planning to get a major loan in that time period. In =
the case of a client preparing to get a mortgage, tackling older accounts m=
ay benefit them no matter how much they are affecting the score as some len=
ders and programs will only allow an applicant to have a certain amount of =
negative debt reporting. This amount is usually under $1,000.</span><br sty=
